          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong prime ideal theorem
            is a theorem stating that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+ideal"} proper ideal
            is contained in some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideal"} prime ideal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+principle"} ultrafilter principle
            (UF), a weak form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            (AC).
          %p We say ‘a’ prime ideal theorem (PIT) instead of ‘the’ prime ideal theorem, since we have not said what the ideals are in. We list some representative examples of prime ideal theorems, all of which are equivalent to (UF) in ZF or even in BZ (bounded Zermelo set theory):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The PIT for (commutative)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            %li
              %p
                The PIT for (bounded)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattices"} distributive lattices
            %li
              %p
                The PIT for
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
            %li
              %p
                The PIT for
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigs"} rigs
          %p
            The usual way to prove a prime ideal theorem is with the help of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+Lemma"} Zorn's Lemma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
            can be used to prove a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+ideal+theorem"} maximal ideal theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideal+theorem"} prime ideal theorem
            becomes a corollary.
          %p
            There are stronger forms of prime ideal theorems which assert the existence of a prime ideal that does not intersect a given multiplicatively closed subset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{m}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{m}
          %h2#a_ladder_of_prime_ideal_theorems A ladder of prime ideal theorems
          %p Prime ideal theorems may be arranged as a sequence of results and techniques that gradually increase in sophistication; we sketch one possible development here, filling in details in the remainder of the article.
          %p
            A first easy wave of results concerns the equivalence between the Boolean PIT (or BPIT) and UF. While these are easy, the BPIT is a theoretical underpinning of fundamental techniques such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactness+theorem"} compactness theorem
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
          %p
            The next step derives the PIT for distributive lattices as a simple application of the compactness theorem. In effect we write down a simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+theory"} propositional theory
            where a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            is tantamount to a prime ideal in a given distributive lattice. Part of the work involves checking finite satisfiability of the theory, where we may invoke a simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
            between finite distributive lattices and finite posets to get the job done.
          %p
            With the PIT for distributive lattices in hand, we prove a key result due to Banaschewski, which may be summarized roughly as saying that a (nontrivial) compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            admits a prime element. This is an early result in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+Spaces"} Stone Spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
          %p
            Banaschewski’s lemma is a key of entry into prime ideal theorems of general type. The general idea is that ideals in a monoid (now in suitably nice monoidal categories) tend to form compact
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantales"} quantales
            %em à la
            Banaschewski’s lemma. As promised, this gives prime ideal theorems of fairly general type (call them collectively “GPIT”).
          %p As suggested earlier, any one of these prime ideal theorems implies the BPIT as a special case, and so we come full circle:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi UF
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi BPIT
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi PIT
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi DistLat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi Banaschewski
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi GPIT
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi BPIT
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi UF
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} UF \Rightarrow BPIT \Rightarrow PIT(DistLat) \Rightarrow Banaschewski \Rightarrow GPIT \Rightarrow BPIT \Rightarrow UF.
          %p We now turn to details.
          %h3#boolean_pit_and_the_ultrafilter_principle Boolean PIT and the ultrafilter principle
          %p
            The Boolean prime ideal theorem or BPIT is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter+principle"} ultrafilter principle
            UF.
          %p
            The reasoning may be summarized as follows: the ultrafilter principle implies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+theorem"} Tychonoff theorem
            for compact Hausdorff spaces; see this
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+theorem#TyCH"} Remark
            and the argument immediately preceding it. The Tychonoff theorem for compact Hausdorff spaces in turn implies that every Boolean ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            has a maximal (and therefore prime) ideal; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+theorem#nonempty"} here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            of a Boolean algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B/I
            has a prime ideal
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^{-1}(P) \subseteq B
            of the quotient map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: B \to B/I
            produces a prime ideal in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            which contains a given ideal
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
          %p
            (In passing, one may note that prime ideals in Boolean algebras (or Boolean rings)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are the same as maximal ideals. For, the corresponding Boolean quotient ring is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
            only if it is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/(2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+element"} idempotency
            of elements yields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e(1-e) = 0
            and then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = 0
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = 1
            assuming no
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+divisors"} zero divisors
          %p
            Finally, maximal ideals are complementary to ultrafilters (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra#aremax"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = P X
            is contained in an ultrafilter, translates into a special case of the BPIT: every ideal of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X
            (consisting of negations of elements of a corresponding filter) is contained in a prime/maximal ideal. This brings us full circle: BPIT implies UF implies Tychonoff(CH) implies BPIT.
          %h3#bpit_implies_prime_ideal_theorem_for_distributive_lattices BPIT implies prime ideal theorem for distributive lattices
          %p
            Now let us prove that the Tychonoff theorem for CH spaces implies the PIT for distributive lattices
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is contained in a prime ideal of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D/I
            has a prime ideal
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^{-1}(P)
            of a prime ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            along the quotient map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: D \to D/I
            is again prime.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              (UF) Any nontrivial distributive lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              has a prime ideal
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            To prove this, we write down a propositional theory of “a prime ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lindenbaum+algebra"} Lindenbaum algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(U D)
            freely generated by the underlying set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U D
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U D
            corresponds to a generator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Bool
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_x \in Bool(U D)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi x
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_x =
            “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in P
            ”. The conditions that enforce “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a prime ideal of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is closed under finite joins)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_0
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi a
                        %mo ∧
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi b
                        %mo ⇒
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_a \wedge P_b \Rightarrow P_{a \vee b}
            %li
              %p
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is downward closed) For each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in U D
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_a \Rightarrow P_{a \wedge b}
            %li
              %p
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is proper)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg P_1
            %li
              %p
                (primality condition)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi b
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi a
                        %mo ∨
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{a \wedge b} \Rightarrow (P_a \vee P_b)
          %p
            These axioms (certain elements of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Bool
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(U D)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is proper (“finite satisfiability of the theory”), the BPIT ensures that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is contained in an ultrafilter
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            or a Boolean ring homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(U D)/\mathcal{F} \to \mathbf{2}
            out of the Lindenbaum algebra. For that model, the collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi D
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a \in D: P_a \in \mathcal{U}\}
            then forms a prime ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              (The filter
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
              is proper.) To see that the filter generated by finitely many axioms does not contain
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Bool
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in Bool(U D)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              generated by the finitely many elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              named in atomic subformulas of such axioms. A finitely generated distributive lattice is finite (indeed, by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              or elements is isomorphic to the poset of downward-closed subsets of the poset
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 2
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}^n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              has a prime ideal. This also follows from Stone duality: a distributive lattice homomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \to \mathbf{2}
              corresponds to a point of the Stone dual
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is nontrivial then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              must be inhabited by a point
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \to \mathbf{2}
              exist and the kernel of such is a maximal and therefore prime ideal.
          %h3#banaschewskis_lemma Banaschewski’s lemma
          %p
            During the 1980’s,
            %a{:href => "#Ban"} Banaschewski
            discovered the following result (a breakthrough in the study of prime ideal theorems, according to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.researchgate.net/publication/242573716_Prime_Ideal_Theorems_and_systems_of_finite_character"} Marcel Erné
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Banaschewski’s lemma)
              Every nontrivial distributive complete lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              whose top element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+element"} compact
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
          .num_remark
            %h6#remarks Remarks
            %p
              Here “nontrivial” means
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \neq 1
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/too+simple+to+be+simple"} too simple to be simple
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is
              %em prime
              if the principal ideal it generates is prime, i.e., if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \wedge b \leq p
              implies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq p
              or
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = a \wedge b
              implies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = a
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = b
              (meet-irreducibility).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              First we observe that a coproduct of nontrivial bounded distributive lattices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X_i\}_{i \in I}
              (in the category of bounded distributive lattices) is also nontrivial. Again this is a kind of finite satisfiability result: if such a coproduct were trivial, i.e., if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 = 1
              were satisfied in the coproduct (constructed syntactically as the free distributive lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = F(\sum_i X_i)
              modulo equations that identify finite meets and joins in each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              with formal meets and joins in
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 = 1
              would be derivable from finitely many equations involving finitely many elements
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              in the
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 = 1
              would be derivable in a finite coproduct of finite bounded nontrivial distributive lattices generated by these elements. This cannot happen, by considering their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+duality"} Stone duality
              with finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
              and products thereof: finite products of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
              finite posets are also inhabited.
            %p
              With that in hand, consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi L
                        %mo ∖
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = \sum_{a \in L \setminus \{1\}} \uparrow a
              of all the nontrivial principal filters of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_a: \uparrow a \to M
              denote the coproduct inclusion.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              has a prime ideal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              by the PIT for distributive lattices; the pullback
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi a
                    %mo ≔
                    %msubsup
                      %mi i
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_a \coloneqq i_a^{-1}(P)
              is a prime ideal of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \uparrow a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi a
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi L
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_a \subseteq L \setminus \{1\}
              by properness of prime ideals.
            %p
              Put
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mi L
                      %mo ∖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = L \setminus \{1\}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dcpo"} dcpo
              by completeness of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              and compactness of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \subseteq S
              are directed subsets, we see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              admits a sup
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma(a) \coloneqq \bigvee P_a
              for each
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq \sigma(a)
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in S
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Bourbaki-Witt fixed point theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma: S \to S
              has a fixed point, say
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo :
                      %mi c
                      %mo =
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c: c = \sigma(c)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi c
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_c \subseteq \uparrow c
              is just
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{c\}
            %p
              So
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{c\}
              is a prime ideal in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \uparrow c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              is a prime element in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              When the statement is about compact frames, it can be rephrased as saying that every nontrivial compact locale has at least one point; compare
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+Spaces"} Stone Spaces
          %h3#prime_elements_in_quantales_of_ideals Prime elements in quantales of ideals
          %p To show how Banaschewski’s lemma is applied, we consider for example the prime ideal theorem for (possibly noncommutative) rings. We follow the common convention that rings have units.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            be a ring, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Idl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idl(R)
            be the sup-lattice of two-sided ideals of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cdot B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantale"} quantale
            structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Idl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idl(R)
            whose multiplicative unit is the top element
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %em affine
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicartesian+monoidal+category"} semicartesian
          %p
            The top element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Idl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idl(R)
            is also a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+element"} compact element
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %em compact
            if its top element is a compact element.
          %p
            Finally, in a quantale
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in Q
            is
            %em prime
            if for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in Q
            we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a b \leq p
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leq p
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Idl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idl(R)
            are precisely prime ideals of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          #quantale.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p (ZF + UF) Every nontrivial compact affine quantale has a prime element.
          %p
            The strategy (following
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Pas"} Paseka
          %p
            We begin by introducing an equivalence relation on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \equiv b
            if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi c
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\forall_c)\; 1 = a \vee c \Leftrightarrow 1 = b \vee c
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              The relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≡
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv
              is a quantale congruence.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              First we show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≡
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv
              respects the quantale multiplication
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⋅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \equiv b
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \equiv y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi a
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq a x \vee c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq a \vee c
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq x \vee c
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq b \vee c
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq y \vee c
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
